        "type": "p",
        "text": "The word is woven into more of Hebrew than you would guess. *Shema* is a name. *Shim'on* — Simeon — comes from this root and means, roughly, \"God has heard.\" When a Hebrew mother named her son Shim'on, she was making a statement of faith: *He heard me. My cry did not fall into the dark.* Even the plainest modern Hebrew keeps the word alive — *shomea* is \"I hear,\" *lishmoa* is \"to listen,\" and if you tell someone in Israel *tishma* — literally \"hear!\" — you are saying something closer to \"listen, let me tell you something,\" the way we say \"look\" when we mean *pay attention with your whole self.*"
